{
  "raw": {
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/as9qa/Senior-Future-Student-Coordinator,34164": {
      "title": "Senior Future Student Coordinator",
      "description": "Senior Future Student Coordinator -\r\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Senior Future Student Coordinator Te Rōpū Pāpori | Engagement Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand Permanent position, 37.5 hours per week $95,784 - $116,911p.a. 5 weeks annual leave + 5 days university holidays Generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) This is a varied senior operational role for an accomplished administration leader who can organise people, processes and information to help UC deliver an excellent experience for future students in Aotearoa New Zealand and around the world. Āu Mahi | What You Will Do You will provide day-to-day leadership of the Future Students coordination function and line-manage three Future Students Coordinators. You will bring structure, pace and consistency to a broad portfolio of operational administration supporting student recruitment, conversion and engagement, including international recruitment activity. You will be part of the Future Students leadership team, working alongside peer leaders in a collaborative structure. This role combines people leadership with hands-on operational expertise: Lead the daily coordination function, allocating and prioritising work across the team to meet changing demands, deadlines and service expectations. Oversee a varied programme of operational administration spanning recruitment activities, conversion, CRM, communications, events, visits and stakeholder engagement. Ensure processes are policy-driven, well documented and applied consistently, with appropriate controls, records, escalation pathways and attention to risk. Maintain service continuity and quality through clear workflows, cross-training, shared coverage and practical performance standards. Coordinate operational support for international recruitment, including education agent agreements and commissions, partner records, communications and programme administration. Support prospective-student enquiry, conversion and engagement activity, including shared inboxes, campaigns, offer-acceptance initiatives and outsourced services. Coach and develop coordinators, create role clarity, monitor capability and provide constructive guidance on complex or high-priority work. Use data, reporting and frontline insight to identify problems, improve customer experience and introduce more efficient, innovative ways of working. Mōu | Who You Are You are a highly organised and adaptable senior administration or operations professional who enjoys making complex services work well. Detailed and policy-minded, you can move confidently between people leadership, workflow management and hands-on delivery. You bring ideas for improvement, but also the discipline to implement them effectively. You will be able to demonstrate experience in: At least five years’ senior experience in administration, operational coordination, student recruitment, customer service, relationship management, marketing, communications or a related field. Managing or leading people, including allocating work, balancing capacity, setting expectations, coaching performance and building team capability. Applying high-level administrative and coordination skills across multiple operational functions, projects or workstreams simultaneously. Working within policies, procedures and regulatory requirements while maintaining accurate records, documentation, data quality and operational controls. Identifying process improvements and introducing practical innovations, efficiencies, automation or better ways of delivering services. Using CRM systems, databases, digital communication tools and Microsoft Office applications to support workflows, reporting and decision-making. Supporting student recruitment or international education operations, including prospective-student communications, conversion activity, agent processes or stakeholder programmes. Building trusted relationships and communicating clearly with colleagues, students, agents, partners and other internal and external stakeholders. An alignment with our organisation's values and culture, promoting a positive and inclusive work environment. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With Future Students includes domestic student recruitment and international student recruitment. Together the team successfully builds the profile and reputation of UC as an outstanding, world-class learning environment that supports the pursuit of excellence, attracts a diversity of talent and investment and continuously reinforces the value of UC qualifications and experience.  For more information about our team, please visit us here . Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC At UC, being part of our team means more than just a job. We are committed to supporting our kaimahi | staff’s career and wellbeing with benefits designed to help our kaimahi grow and feel connected, supported and valued. 5 weeks annual leave + 5 days university holidays generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) flexible working arrangements professional development opportunities supportive working environment a wide range of retail discounts across shopping, entertainment and travel on-campus staff discounts living in revitalised Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa New Zealand a unique working environment on a beautiful campus, with access to UC facilities such as the UC RecCentre and Staff Club at discounted rates, plus onsite cafés and eateries. For more information, please visit us: https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/about-uc/work-at-uc/benefits-working-uc For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/ The closing date for this position is: Friday16 October 2026 (midnight, NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed as they are received, and interviews may take place before the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted via our careers website and should include a cover letter and resume. Please note, we do not accept applications by email. However, we are happy to answer queries about the application process; please forward these to WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz. You must have Aotearoa New Zealand or Australian citizenship/permanent residency, or hold a valid New Zealand work visa, to be considered for this role. Please note, this role is based in Ōtautahi Christchurch. Job Details Reference # 34164 Posted on 02 Oct 2026 Closes on 16 Oct 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Administration Job level(s) Experienced Work type(s) Continuing (Permanent) full-time More details (document) PD_Senior Future Students Coordinator.pdf Position description 1 Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 18 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/bMbqa/Lecturer-Senior-Lecturer-in-Child-and-Family-Psychology,34170": {
      "title": "Lecturer / Senior Lecturer in Child and Family Psychology",
      "description": "Lecturer / Senior Lecturer in Child and Family Psychology -\r\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Lecturer / Senior Lecturer in Child and Family Psychology Te Kaupeka Oranga| Faculty of Health Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand Full-time 37.5 hours per week (1.0 FTE) or part-time 0.6FTE up to a maximum of 1.0 FTE Starting date: January 2027 Continuing (permanent) position Kia hiwa rā, kia hiwa rā! He hiahia, he pūkenga nōu ki te mahi a te Pūkenga / Pūkenga Matua? Nāia te pōwhiri nā Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha kia tono mai i te tūranga nei. Āu Mahi | What You Will Do The University of Canterbury is seeking to fill a permanent, academic post in its prestigious Child and Family Psychology programme, commencing January 2027. Part-time role/s 0.6FTE up to a maximum of 1.0 FTE may also be considered. The successful candidate will be a talented researcher and a highly experienced Psychologist. We are looking for someone who is dedicated to training our future Child and Family Psychologists; and who shares our programme’s philosophical emphases on cross-sectoral, and culturally sensitive practice, within a scientist-practitioner framework. Mōu | Who You Are The successful candidate will have, or starting to build an active research programme in a clinical child, developmental, family and/or parenting field. The successful candidate will also have significant professional standing as a psychologist working with children and families, including clinical leadership and/or senior advisory experience. The essential selection criteria are: a PhD in Psychology or other relevant discipline (a practising Psychologist working towards completing a PhD in Psychology may be considered, depending on academic and professional experience) registered to practice as a Psychologist in New Zealand, or (for overseas applicants) eligible to be registered in New Zealand at least 5 years post-registration experience as a psychologist working with children, adolescents and/or families, with evidence of significant professional standing in your field of practice experience leading a child and family psychology team or service, or other relevant clinical leadership and/or experience in senior clinical advisory roles research and scholarship in a field relevant to child and family psychological practice and/or policy, consistent with appointment at Lecturer or Senior Lecturer level current experience teaching in a post-graduate Child and Family Psychology training programme that culminates in Psychologist registration / licensure Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With The Child and Family Psychology Programme encompasses a two-year open-entry Master’s degree, followed by a three-year limited-entry Postgraduate Diploma in Child and Family Psychology, which can be completed in four years of full-time postgraduate study. The diploma leads to registration as a Psychologist with the New Zealand Psychologists’ Board. This is a cross-sectoral programme that aims to equip psychologists to work with children and families in varied settings, including Child and Adolescent Mental Health, Paediatrics, Education, Child Welfare (including alternate care and adoption), Disability Services, and various types of non-government children’s services. The programme is unique to New Zealand and Australia, and is one of only a few of its kind in the world. The programme also operates the Pukemanu Centre , a state-of-the art university training clinic that offers considerable scope for carrying out clinical and applied developmental research with children, parents and families. The successful applicant will have opportunities for international conference travel to support their research and teaching activities and will have the opportunity to network with international  colleagues through the University’s unique Erskine Fellowship and Canterbury Fellowship Scheme (which enables the University to host world leading academics) and through the University’s academic staff exchange programmes with Oxford and Cambridge Universities in the UK. For more information about our team and programmes, please visit us at Child and Family Psychology Programme Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC Tangata Tū, Tangata Ora - – Engaged, Empowered, Making a Difference. We are committed to accessible higher education, service to the community and the encouragement of talent without barriers of distance, wealth, class, gender or ethnicity. The University explicitly aims to produce graduates and support staff who are engaged with their communities, empowered to act for good and determined to make a difference in the world. What we offer The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment, this includes: relocation support onsite childcare facilities five weeks annual leave and five days university holidays UC Parental Leave (up to 9 weeks paid) + 26 weeks Government paid parental leave. widely accessible sabbatical provision generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) University and Faculty scholarship support for post-graduate students a unique bequest funded endowment ( Erskine Fund ) that supports an extensive visiting fellowship programme flexible working arrangements supportive working environment professional development and study opportunities living in revitalised Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa New Zealand a unique working environment in a beautiful campus with access to UC facilities such as the recreation centre and Staff Club at discounted rates plus onsite cafés and eateries, and more. For more information on our Benefits, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/joinus/benefits/ The closing date for this position is: 16 October 2026 (midnight NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed as they are received, and interviews may take place before the close date. This role sits within the Academic Staff, Associated Staff and Support Staff Collective Agreement and the following salary scale applies: Lecturer $94,181 - $113,616; Senior Lecturer $119,586 - $135,630. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted on our careers website and should include the following: a 1-page cover letter , a 1-page research statement outlining your research interests and goals, and a 1-page teaching philosophy statement (combined) a Resume which includes a list of relevant research publications (combined). Please note, only 2 documents can be uploaded in the application process. We do not accept applications by email, however, we are happy to answer any queries at WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz For further information specifically about the role, please contact: Senior Lecturer, Sarah Whitcombe-Dobbs sarah.whitcombe-dobbs@canterbury.ac.nz For overseas applicants, please visit Immigration New Zealand website, to review the latest health and visa requirements for visiting and working in New Zealand. #LI-DNI Job Details Reference # 34170 Posted on 02 Oct 2026 Closes on 16 Oct 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Health, Lecturer Job level(s) Academic Work type(s) Continuing (Permanent) full-time More details (document) Position description 1 Lecturer_PD.pdf Position description 2 Senior Lecturer_PD.pdf Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 16 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/nE6pa/Lecturer-Teaching-and-Administration-,33898": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Teaching and Administration)",
      "description": "Lecturer (Teaching and Administration) -\r\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Lecturer (Teaching and Administration) Te Kura Aronukurangi | School of Earth and Environment Te Kaupeka Pūtaiao | Science Faculty Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand Full-time 37.5 hours per week (1.0 FTE) Continuing (permanent) position Generous annual leave provisions and professional development opportunities Āu Mahi | What You Will Do The University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha invites applications for a full-time, continuing (permanent) position at the level of Lecturer – Teaching and Administration in the School of Earth and Environment (SEE) with expertise in environmental science. In this role you will assist with delivery and design of classroom, laboratory, and field teaching. You will deploy lab and field skills to assist students with lab and field work. You will contribute to teaching in a range of courses in the environmental science programmes. Duties will include: Lecturing, support, and coordination of large undergraduate courses; Providing lab and field work support for students; Teaching and supporting undergraduate students’ work in accordance with the policies and procedures of the University of Canterbury and best-practice guidelines developed by SEE; Supporting students’ pastoral care needs and other requirements by connecting them to the relevant University support services; and Assisting and supporting environmental science teaching staff with the University’s online learning management system (LEARN), Course Management System, and other inward- and outward-facing platforms sharing relevant course- or programme-related information. This is an exciting opportunity to advance teaching and administration in environmental science. Mōu | Who You Are You will hold a minimum of an Masters of Science, but ideally a PhD, in environmental science or a closely-related discipline, with proven experience in undertaking and teaching in the lab and in the field. You will bring: Experience running student labs and workshops; Experience and/or willingness to lead and coordinate tutors in large classes. Knowledge of and experience with lab and field techniques, ideally including analysing soils, plants, water quality, and contamination; Evidence of training in the theory and practice of science at a tertiary level; Demonstrated understanding of, and experience in, delivering effective teaching that enables a wide range of learners to succeed; Evidence of reflective learning and continuous professional development; Active commitment to upholding the values, tikanga (protocols and processes), kawa (rules) of cultural practice and traditions as guided by mana whenua, Ngāi Tūāhuriri, and valuing te reo Māori; Ability to collaborate with others within SEE and the wider university, and contribute to School, faculty, and university activities; Alignment with the University of Canterbury's values and culture, promoting a positive and inclusive work environment; and Willingness to mentor and support student projects is required; experience doing so is desirable. Desirable skills you could bring include: Tertiary pedagogical training (e,g,, Fellow of the Higher Education Authority) Competency in te reo Māori me ōna tikanga; Experience with Transnational Education, and international students; Mandarin spoken and written; and Industry and public sector engagement. If you are passionate about advancing environmental science and ready to make a significant contribution to teaching, course organisation and supporting Tauira in environmental science and the School of Earth and Environment, we encourage you to apply and join us in shaping the future of environmental science at UC. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With Te Kura Aronukurangi | School of Earth and Environment is dedicated to excellence in both research and teaching with collaborative links with research groups and industry throughout New Zealand and internationally. Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC Tangata Tū, Tangata Ora - – Engaged, Empowered, Making a Difference. We are committed to accessible higher education, service to the community and the encouragement of talent without barriers of distance, wealth, class, gender or ethnicity. The University explicitly aims to produce graduates and support staff who are engaged with their communities, empowered to act for good and determined to make a difference in the world. Mea e tukua nei | What we offer The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment, this includes: five weeks annual leave and five days university holidays UC Parental Leave (up to 9 weeks paid) + 26 weeks Government paid parental leave and onsite childcare facility generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) flexible working arrangements supportive working environment professional development and study opportunities living in revitalised Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa New Zealand a unique working environment in a beautiful campus with access to UC facilities such as the recreation centre and Staff Club at discounted rates plus onsite cafés and eateries, and more. For more information on our Benefits, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/joinus/benefits/ For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit www.canterbury.ac.nz This role sits within the Academic Staff, Associated Staff and Support Staff Collective Agreement and the following salary scale applies $68,880p.a (1.01) - $98,800p.a. (3.01). The closing date for this position is: 28 October 2026 (midnight NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed after the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted on our careers website and should include the following: a cover letter that includes a 2–3-page statement outlining your teaching philosophy (combined) a CV that includes teaching, lab, and field experience and any research publications if applicable. We do not accept applications by email, however, we are happy to answer any queries at WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz For further information specifically about the role, please contact: Jamie Shulmeister (Selection Committee Chair) at james.shulmeister@canterbury.ac.nz . For overseas applicants, please visit Immigration New Zealand website, to review the latest health and visa requirements for visiting and working in New Zealand. Please note, this is an onsite position. Job Details Reference # 33898 Posted on 09 Oct 2026 Closes on 28 Oct 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Education & Training, Lecturer Job level(s) Academic Work type(s) Continuing (Permanent) full-time More details (document) PD_Lecturer (TA) SEE.pdf Position description 1 Senior Tutor Position Description.pdf Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 22 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/0nKqa/Lecturer-Senior-Lecturer-in-M-ori-or-Indigenous-Business,34261": {
      "title": "Lecturer / Senior Lecturer in Māori or Indigenous Business",
      "description": "Lecturer / Senior Lecturer in Māori or Indigenous Business -\r\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Lecturer / Senior Lecturer in Māori or Indigenous Business Te Kura Umanga | UC Business School Ōtautahi Christchurch, Aotearoa New Zealand Kia hiw a rā, kia hiwa rā! E te ruru e koukou nei! He hiahia, he pūkenga nōu i ngā kaupapa umanga, mātauranga a-iwi, a-iwi taketake rānei? Nāia te pōwhiri nā Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha kia tono mai te hunga i te tūranga Pūkenga / Pūkenga Matua ki Te Kura Umanga. Full-time at 37.5 hours per week (1.0 FTE) Continuing (i.e. permanent) position Āu Mahi | What You Will Do Te Kura Umanga | UC Business School invites applications for a continuing position of Pūkenga | Lecturer, Pūkenga Matua | Senior Lecturer in Māori or Indigenous Business. The successful candidate will be able to contribute to: Research and teaching activities for areas covered by UCBS in relation to Māori or Indigenous perspectives on UCBS disciplines. A successful candidate will have expertise as follows: A PhD in a business or relevant social science discipline, desirable. Candidates working towards a PhD in a relevant field, will also be considered. Expertise in Māori or Indigenous perspectives on business, society, the environment A publication track record as well as experience with preparation and delivery of quality oral presentations to a wide range of audiences A proven ability to communicate complex ideas and concepts (both orally and in writing) to a wide variety or groups including policy makers, communities and academics An aptitude for, and ideally experience of, community engagement in research. The level of appointment will depend on the successful candidate’s qualifications and experience. A primary focus of the appointment is to teach and research in areas relevant to UCBS disciplines. Evidence of gaining external research funding for Māori related studies would be welcome. The School also has a variety of teaching and research needs across discipline areas to increase engagement with Te Ao Māori and bicultural competence and confidence. Mōu | Who You Are You will be a highly motivated academic or practitioner who wishes to move into academia. At Lecturer level, you should demonstrate the promise of high-quality teaching and research. At Senior Lecturer level, you should have a proven record of high-quality research and teaching. You will also demonstrate academic leadership across the University, taking a lead in School and University committees, and initiatives that support the achievement of UC strategic and operational objectives. The School encourages applications from Māori business academics that can demonstrate Treaty partnership or intercultural competence and confidence. Te Kura Umanga | UC Business School at Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, is committed to graduating ākonga with cultural competence and confidence. Applicants should have an advanced qualification in Business or substantial industry experience that aligns with the areas of interest with a genuine interest to pursue an academic career. Applicants will ideally have research-level knowledge in one or more of the specified areas, a record of research productivity, or clear potential. A high level of understanding of treaty partnership, tikanga and te reo Māori, combined with an ability to work and teach in a culturally responsive way is critical to the role. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With Te Kura Umanga | UC Business School holds the gold standard ‘triple-crown’ of international business school accreditations having achieved EQUIS, AACSB and AMBA accreditations. Only 1% (fewer than 100) of business schools worldwide hold the ‘triple crown’ which is a recognition of the high performance and quality of research and programmes offered by the School. Our programmes, research teaching, and facilities have undergone meticulous review by each of the international organisations offering these accreditations. We offer an extensive range of undergraduate and postgraduate qualifications that prepare you to be work-ready in a diverse world. Our courses are internationally recognised, underpinned by leading-edge research in a vibrant learning environment. For more information about Te Kura Umanga visit www.canterbury.ac.nz/business Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC Tangata Tū, Tangata Ora - – Engaged, Empowered, Making a Difference. We are committed to accessible higher education, service to the community and the encouragement of talent without barriers of distance, wealth, class, gender or ethnicity. The University explicitly aims to produce graduates and support staff who are engaged with their communities, empowered to act for good and determined to make a difference in the world. Mea e tukua nei | What we offer The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment, this includes: relocation support five weeks annual leave and five days university holidays UC Parental Leave (up to 9 weeks paid) + 26 weeks Government paid parental leave and onsite childcare facility widely accessible sabbatical provision generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) University and Faculty scholarship support for post-graduate students a unique bequest funded endowment ( Erskine Fund ) that supports an extensive visiting fellowship programme flexible working arrangements supportive working environment professional development and study opportunities living in revitalised Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa New Zealand a unique working environment in a beautiful campus with access to UC facilities such as the recreation centre and Staff Club at discounted rates plus onsite cafés and eateries, and more. For more information on our Benefits, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/joinus/benefits/ This role sits within the Academic Staff, Associated Staff and Support Staff Collective Agreement and the following salary scale applies: Lecturer $94,181 - $113,616; Senior Lecturer $119,586 - $135,630. The closing date for this position is: 1 November 2026 (midnight NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed after the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted on our careers website and should include the following: a 1-page cover letter , a 1-page research statement outlining your research interests and goals, and a 1-page teaching philosophy statement (combined) a CV which includes a list of relevant research publications (combined). Please note, only 2 documents can be uploaded in the application process. We do not accept applications by email, however, we are happy to answer any queries at WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz For further information specifically about the role, please contact: Ellie Norris, Associate Director, MBA Programme, ellie.norris@canterbury.ac.nz For overseas applicants, please visit Immigration New Zealand website, to review the latest health and visa requirements for visiting and working in New Zealand. Please note, this is an onsite position. Job Details Reference # 34261 Posted on 08 Oct 2026 Closes on 01 Nov 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Senior Lecturer Job level(s) Academic Work type(s) Continuing (Permanent) full-time More details (document) Position description 1 Lecturer.pdf Position description 2 Senior Lecturer.pdf Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 22 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/n7qqa/MBA-Coordinator,34204": {
      "title": "MBA Coordinator",
      "description": "MBA Coordinator -\r\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search MBA Coordinator Te Kura Umanga | UC Business School Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand Permanent position, 37.5 hours per week 5 weeks annual leave +5 days university holidays Professional development opportunities Generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) Kia hiwa rā, kia hiwa rā! He hiahia, he pūkenga nōu ki te mahi a te MBA Coordinator ? Nāia te pōwhiri nā Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha kia tono mai i te tūranga nei. Āu Mahi | What You Will Do Bring together people, programmes and opportunities across UC’s Master of Business Administration (MBA), providing trusted advice to prospective and current students while playing a pivotal role in recruitment and admission, academic administration, industry engagement and the day-to-day coordination that keeps the MBA experience seamless and engaging. From industry engagement events and student workshops to block weekends and other programme activities, you will coordinate experiences that bring the MBA to life, while building strong relationships across the Business School, wider University, industry and the MBA community. Mōu | Who You Are You are an experienced administrator who enjoys working in a busy, people-focused environment and takes pride in delivering accurate, proactive and high-quality support. You will bring: 5–6 years’ experience in an administrative support role, preferably within a large organisation. Tertiary experience or an understanding of the New Zealand education system would be advantageous. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills, with a strong customer and student focus. Excellent organisational skills with the ability to manage competing priorities, deadlines and high-volume administrative activity. Strong attention to detail and a high level of accuracy, with the ability to manage confidential information appropriately. Confidence using modern office technology, Microsoft Office applications and databases, and a readiness to quickly learn new systems. The ability to work independently, exercise sound judgement and collaborate effectively with a wide range of people. A commitment to UC’s values, inclusive practice and working respectfully within tikanga Māori and Te Tiriti o Waitangi. Competency in te reo Māori me ōna tikanga would be an advantage. An alignment with our organisation's values and culture, promoting a positive and inclusive work environment. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With You will report to the MBA Director and work closely with the MBA team, academics, the Associate Dean (Academic), Academic Services Manager, recruitment and marketing teams, and a wide range of Business School and University service areas. This role involves building strong relationships with MBA students, graduates, industry partners, suppliers and other programme stakeholders, playing a key role in creating a professional, welcoming and connected MBA community. Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC At UC, being part of our team means more than just a job. We are committed to supporting our kaimahi | staff’s career and wellbeing with benefits designed to help our kaimahi grow and feel connected, supported and valued. 5 weeks annual leave +5 days university holidays generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) flexible working arrangements professional development opportunities supportive working environment a wide range of retail discounts across shopping, entertainment and travel on-campus staff discounts living in revitalised Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa New Zealand a unique working environment in a beautiful campus with access to UC facilities such as UC RecCentre and Staff Club at discounted rates plus onsite cafés and eateries, and more. For more information, please visit us: https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/about-uc/work-at-uc/benefits-working-uc For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/ The closing date for this position is: 21 October 2026 (midnight, NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed as they are received, and interviews may take place before the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted via our careers website and should include a cover letter and CV. Please note, we do not accept applications by email, however we are happy to answer your queries in relation to the application process, please forward these to WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz You must have Aotearoa New Zealand or Australian citizenship/permanent residency to be considered for this role. Please note, this is an on-site position. Job Details Reference # 34204 Posted on 07 Oct 2026 Closes on 21 Oct 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Administration Job level(s) Experienced Work type(s) Continuing (Permanent) full-time More details (document) MBA Coordinator_PD.pdf Position description 1 Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 22 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/Ajaqa/Senior-Commercialisation-Advisor,34168": {
      "title": "Senior / Commercialisation Advisor",
      "description": "Senior / Commercialisation Advisor -\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Senior / Commercialisation Advisor Te Rōpū Rangahau | Research & Innovation Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand Two, fixed-term 12 month positions Full-time, 37.5 hours per week 4 weeks annual leave + 5 days university holidays Make a direct contribution to the real-world impact of UC research Āu Mahi | What You Will Do Turn research breakthroughs into products, services, licences and ventures that create meaningful economic and social benefit. We are recruiting for two fixed-term roles, a Senior Commercialisation Advisor and a Commercialisation Advisor, offering the opportunity to match the scope of the role to your experience. Working alongside researchers, professional advisers, investors and industry partners, you will: Assess research discoveries and intellectual property with researchers, subject-matter experts and professional advisers. Undertake market, competitor and potential licensee research, including opportunity sizing, valuation and commercial analysis. Support intellectual property strategy, patentability and freedom-to-operate analysis with patent attorneys and other specialists. Recommend and progress appropriate knowledge-transfer pathways, including licensing and new venture creation. Develop compelling business cases and investment proposals, and manage funded projects and reporting. Support due diligence, commercial negotiations, and the drafting and management of agreements. Build productive relationships with KiwiNet, the Commercialisation Partner Network, Callaghan Innovation technology incubators, investors and industry. Help researchers articulate credible commercialisation and impact pathways for funding proposals, including MBIE opportunities. Educate, advise and inspire UC staff about intellectual property and the benefits of commercialising innovation. Commercialisation Advisor: You will support and manage innovation projects across the commercialisation lifecycle, applying sound analysis, project management and relationship skills to help move opportunities forward. Senior Commercialisation Advisor: You will bring greater depth of commercialisation experience, independently lead complex opportunities, shape portfolio and investment decisions, provide senior-level advice, and deputise for the IP & Commercialisation Manager when required. When applying online you will be asked to indicate which role(s) you are applying for. Mōu | Who You Are You are commercially curious, analytical and collaborative. You can move confidently between technical detail, market evidence and stakeholder relationships, and you are motivated by helping ideas achieve impact beyond the University. You will be able to demonstrate experience in: Technology, product or service commercialisation, investment proposals, start-ups, technology companies, research organisations or commercialisation service providers. Technology or product development, including market and commercialisation planning. Intellectual property management and protection mechanisms. Project management and the development of persuasive business cases or investment proposals. Communicating complex information clearly, with the confidence to influence, negotiate and advise a wide range of people. A relevant Bachelor’s degree in Science or Engineering (NZQA Level 8), or a business or legal qualification combined with relevant industry experience. Using Microsoft Office, database applications and research tools effectively. For appointment at Senior level, demonstrated experience leading complex commercialisation projects, securing investment, managing external networks and providing authoritative advice. An alignment with our organisation’s values and culture, promoting a positive and inclusive work environment. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With You will join Te Rōpū Rangahau | Research & Innovation and work closely with the IP & Commercialisation Manager, commercialisation advisers, UC researchers and research funding colleagues. You will also engage with external stakeholders such as other university commercialisation offices, industry contacts, investors, funders and specialist advisers. Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC At UC, being part of our team means more than just a job. We are committed to supporting our kaimahi | staff’s career and wellbeing with benefits designed to help our kaimahi grow and feel connected, supported and valued. 4 weeks annual leave +5 days university holidays generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) flexible working arrangement professional development opportunities supportive working environment a wide range of retail discounts across shopping, entertainment and travel on-campus staff discounts living in revitalised Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa New Zealand a unique working environment in a beautiful campus with access to UC facilities such as UC RecCentre and Staff Club at discounted rates plus onsite cafés and eateries, and more. For more information, please visit us: https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/about-uc/work-at-uc/benefits-working-uc For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/ The closing date for these positions is: Sunday 18 October 2026 (midnight, NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed as they are received, and interviews may take place before the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications must be submitted through our careers website and should include a cover letter and CV. Please indicate whether you wish to be considered for the Commercialisation Advisor role, the Senior Commercialisation Advisor role, or both. Candidates may be considered at the level that best matches their experience and demonstrated capability. Please note, we do not accept applications by email. For queries about the application process, contact WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz. You must have Aotearoa New Zealand or Australian citizenship/permanent residency or hold a valid NZ work visa to be considered for this role. The roles are based onsite. Job Details Reference # 34168 Posted on 07 Oct 2026 Closes on 18 Oct 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Project Management, Research & Development, Science & Technology Job level(s) Experienced Work type(s) Fixed term full-time More details (document) PDs_Commericalisation_Snr Commercilisation Advisor.pdf Position description 1 Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 22 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance. Loading . . ."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/E7aqa/Commercialisation-Analyst,34166": {
      "title": "Commercialisation Analyst",
      "description": "Commercialisation Analyst -\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Commercialisation Analyst Te Rōpū Rangahau | Research & Innovation Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand Fixed-term, 12 month position Full-time, 37.5 hours per week, part-time will be considered 4 weeks annual leave + 5 days university holidays Āu Mahi | What You Will Do Help turn promising research into opportunities that benefit Aotearoa New Zealand. In this fixed-term role as Commercialisation Analyst, you will support the early stages of University of Canterbury (UC)’s commercialisation pipeline, working with researchers and experienced commercialisation professionals you will: Identify and assess invention disclosures through market research, technology landscaping and opportunity analysis. Help prepare business cases, investment proposals, research funding applications, marketing materials and pitches. Gather and interpret market, competitor, customer and industry information to support commercial decisions. Build professional relationships with UC researchers and stakeholders across the commercialisation ecosystem. Support commercialisation systems, project pipelines and team initiatives. Develop practical experience and commercial acumen across intellectual property and technology transfer. Mōu | Who You Are You are an analytical, commercially curious and collaborative professional who enjoys learning about new technologies and translating complex information into clear, practical insights. This is an excellent opportunity to deepen your capability in market validation, intellectual property, relationship management and technology transfer. You will also be able to demonstrate: Professional experience (at least 18 months) in technology, product or service commercialisation, or a closely related area Interest in business, entrepreneurship, commercialisation, marketing or innovation. The ability to understand research and technology across a range of disciplines Strong research, analysis, written communication and stakeholder-engagement skills Confidence using Microsoft Office applications, including Word, Excel and PowerPoint Holding a Bachelor’s degree with honours in Science or Engineering (NZQA Level 8); a Master’s degree in Business or another relevant postgraduate qualification is preferred. An alignment with UC’s values and culture, promoting a positive and inclusive work environment. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With You will join Te Rōpū Rangahau | Research & Innovation and work closely with the IP & Commercialisation Manager, commercialisation advisers, UC researchers and research funding colleagues. You will also engage with external stakeholders such as other university commercialisation offices, industry contacts, investors, funders and specialist advisers. Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC At UC, being part of our team means more than just a job. We are committed to supporting our kaimahi | staff’s career and wellbeing with benefits designed to help our kaimahi grow and feel connected, supported and valued. 4 weeks annual leave +5 days university holidays generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) flexible working arrangement professional development opportunities supportive working environment a wide range of retail discounts across shopping, entertainment and travel on-campus staff discounts living in revitalised Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa New Zealand a unique working environment in a beautiful campus with access to UC facilities such as UC RecCentre and Staff Club at discounted rates plus onsite cafés and eateries, and more. For more information, please visit us: https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/about-uc/work-at-uc/benefits-working-uc For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/ The closing date for this position is: Sunday 18 October 2026 (midnight, NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed as they are received, and interviews may take place before the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted through our careers website and should include a cover letter and CV. In your cover letter, please tell us about your interest in research commercialisation and the transferable skills you would bring. Please note, we do not accept applications by email. For queries about the application process, contact WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz. You must have Aotearoa New Zealand or Australian citizenship/permanent residency or hold a valid NZ work visa to be considered for this role. The roles are based onsite. Job Details Reference # 34166 Posted on 07 Oct 2026 Closes on 18 Oct 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Research & Development Job level(s) Entry/Graduate Work type(s) Fixed term full-time More details (document) PD_Commercialisation_Analyst.pdf Position description 1 Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 22 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance. Loading . . ."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/Qk7qa/Engagement-Advisor-Commercialisation-,34157": {
      "title": "Engagement Advisor (Commercialisation)",
      "description": "Engagement Advisor (Commercialisation) -\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Engagement Advisor (Commercialisation) Te Rōpū Rangahau | Research & Innovation Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand Fixed-term, 12 month position Part-time, 18.75 hours per week 4 weeks annual leave + 5 days university holidays Āu Mahi | What You Will Do Bring people, ideas and opportunities together to help UC research achieve real-world impact. In this fixed-term role as Engagement Advisor (Commercialisation), you will raise awareness of the Research & Innovation (R&S) Commercialisation team, build strong connections across the University of Canterbury (UC) and the wider innovation ecosystem by: Planning and delivering workshops and events focused on commercialisation, entrepreneurship and industry engagement, including leading UC’s annual entrepreneurship and innovation competition. Building strong relationships with academic staff, research groups, postgraduate students and student organisations, maintaining regular connections across UC’s faculties, departments and schools. Identifying and encouraging researchers and students with entrepreneurial ambitions, helping to grow a strong pipeline of commercialisation, consulting and research-industry opportunities. Developing relationships with industry groups, funders, incubators and other university technology transfer offices, and represent the Commercialisation team at external events and forums. Designing targeted engagement strategies and work plans, prioritising effort for maximum impact and managing milestones, timelines, stakeholders, risks and resources. Creating and promoting compelling digital content, newsletters, case studies and success stories in partnership with UC Communications and specialist providers. Mōu | Who You Are You are a proactive, relationship-focused communicator who enjoys connecting people and turning ideas into well-planned engagement activity. You are equally comfortable organising an event, developing a story, presenting to a group and building trusted relationships with researchers, students and external partners. You will also be able to demonstrate experience in: Relationship management, marketing, events or communications (three to five years’ experience), ideally within a tertiary education, research or innovation environment. Building and maintaining productive relationships with a wide range of stakeholders, including researchers, students and external partners. Planning and delivering workshops, competitions, campaigns or other engagement events. Creating and promoting effective digital content and communications, such as newsletters, social media content and case studies. A genuine enthusiasm for entrepreneurship and innovation, with confident presentation and communication skills. Developing work plans, setting priorities and following through independently; a Bachelor’s degree or equivalent experience in entrepreneurship, marketing or communications. An alignment with UC’s values and culture, promoting a positive, inclusive and collaborative work environment. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With You will join Te Rōpū Rangahau | Research & Innovation and work closely with the IP & Commercialisation Manager, commercialisation advisers, UC researchers and research funding colleagues. You will also engage with external stakeholders such as other university commercialisation offices, industry contacts, investors, funders and specialist advisers. Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC At UC, being part of our team means more than just a job. We are committed to supporting our kaimahi | staff’s career and wellbeing with benefits designed to help our kaimahi grow and feel connected, supported and valued. 4 weeks annual leave +5 days university holidays generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) flexible working arrangement professional development opportunities supportive working environment a wide range of retail discounts across shopping, entertainment and travel on-campus staff discounts living in revitalised Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa New Zealand a unique working environment in a beautiful campus with access to UC facilities such as UC RecCentre and Staff Club at discounted rates plus onsite cafés and eateries, and more. For more information, please visit us: https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/about-uc/work-at-uc/benefits-working-uc For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/ The closing date for this position is: Sunday 18 October 2026 (midnight, NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed as they are received, and interviews may take place before the closing date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted through our careers website and should include a cover letter and CV. In your cover letter, please tell us about your experience building engagement and relationships, and your interest in helping research and innovation achieve real-world impact. Please note, we do not accept applications by email. For queries about the application process, contact WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz. You must have Aotearoa New Zealand or Australian citizenship or permanent residency, or hold a valid New Zealand work visa, to be considered for this role. The roles are based onsite. Job Details Reference # 34157 Posted on 07 Oct 2026 Closes on 18 Oct 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Administration, Communications, Marketing, Project Management Job level(s) Experienced Work type(s) Fixed term part-time More details (document) PD_Engagement Advisor.pdf Position description 1 Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 22 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance. Loading . . ."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/m2Hpa/Lecturer-Teaching-and-Administration-,34020": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Teaching and Administration)",
      "description": "Lecturer (Teaching and Administration) -\r\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Lecturer (Teaching and Administration) Te Kura Aronukurangi | School of Earth and Environment Te Kaupeka Pūtaiao | Science Faculty Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand Full-time 37.5 hours per week (1.0 FTE) Continuing (permanent) position Āu Mahi | What You Will Do The University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha invites applications for a full-time, continuing (permanent) academic position at the level of Lecturer (Teaching and Admin only) in the School of Earth and Environment (SEE) with expertise in the area of Geology. In this role you will develop and deliver classroom and field teaching at undergraduate level, potentially co-supervise postgraduate students, and foster collaboration within SEE and with national / international partners. You will contribute to teaching of the Geology and Earth Science degree programmes. This is an exciting opportunity to advance undergraduate teaching in geology and earth systems science. Mōu | Who You Are Applicants having a MSc or PhD in Geology / Earth Science, or closely related discipline, with proven experience in undertaking and teaching introductory and field geology. You will bring: Broad expertise in core geology topics; An extensive understanding of New Zealand geology and earth science is essential Ability to lead, deliver, and coordinate teaching delivery of 1 st year geology / earth science lectures and lab classes Proven leadership in collaborative teaching and academic programme delivery Proven leadership in, or capacity to develop, extended fieldwork teaching programmes at undergraduate level Capability to develop highest quality mentoring and co-supervision of postgraduate students in geology / earth science Ability to collaborate with others within the school and wider university, along with an interest in contributing to school, faculty, and university activities Capacity and interest in “geology / earth science” public out-reach particularly school children An alignment with our organisation’s values and culture, promoting a positive and inclusive work environment. If you are passionate about geology and earth science and ready to make a significant contribution to teaching, we encourage you to apply and join us in shaping the future of SEE. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With Te Kura Aronukurangi | School of Earth and Environment is dedicated to excellence in both research and teaching with collaborative links with research groups and industry throughout New Zealand and internationally. Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC Tangata Tū, Tangata Ora -– Engaged, Empowered, Making a Difference. We are committed to accessible higher education, service to the community and the encouragement of talent without barriers of distance, wealth, class, gender or ethnicity. The University explicitly aims to produce graduates and support staff who are engaged with their communities, empowered to act for good and determined to make a difference in the world. What we offer The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment, this includes: five weeks annual leave and five days university holidays UC Parental Leave (up to 9 weeks paid) + 26 weeks Government paid parental leave and onsite childcare facility generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) flexible working arrangements supportive working environment professional development and study opportunities living in revitalised Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa New Zealand a unique working environment in a beautiful campus with access to UC facilities such as the recreation centre and Staff Club at discounted rates plus onsite cafés and eateries, and more. For more information on our Benefits, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/joinus/benefits/ For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit www.canterbury.ac.nz This role sits within the Academic Staff, Associated Staff and Support Staff Collective Agreement and within the salary range $70,258p.a. to $100,776p.a. dependant on experience. The closing date for this position is: 22nd October 2026 (midnight NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed after the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted on our careers website and should include the following: a cover letter which includes a 2–3-page statement outlining your teaching philosophy and experience a Resume which includes details of your geological interests and teaching experience. We do not accept applications by email, however, we are happy to answer any queries at WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz For further information specifically about the role, please contact: Professor Catherine Reid - catherine.reid@canterbury.ac.nz For overseas applicants, please visit Immigration New Zealand website, to review the latest health and visa requirements for working in New Zealand. Please note, this is an onsite position. Job Details Reference # 34020 Posted on 07 Oct 2026 Closes on 22 Oct 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Education & Training Job level(s) Academic Work type(s) Continuing (Permanent) full-time More details (document) Position description 1 PD_Lecturer (TA)_SEE.pdf Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 22 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/EVsqa/Research-Associate-Postdoctoral-Fellow-in-Gravitational-Microlensing,34206": {
      "title": "Research Associate/Postdoctoral Fellow in Gravitational Microlensing",
      "description": "Research Associate/Postdoctoral Fellow in Gravitational Microlensing -\r\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Research Associate/Postdoctoral Fellow in Gravitational Microlensing Te Kaupeka Pūtaiao | Faculty of Science Te Kura Matū | School of Physical and Chemical Sciences Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand Full-time 37.5 hours per week (1.0 FTE) Fixed term position (2.25 years) Kia hiwa rā, kia hiwa rā! He hiahia, he pūkenga nōu ki te mahi a te Postdoctoral Fellow? Nāia te pōwhiri nā Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha kia tono mai i te tūranga nei. Āu Mahi | What You Will Do The successful applicant will be expected to carry out research in collaboration with Associate Professor Michael Albrow, students, and external colleagues on the analysis of gravitational microlensing data from the NASA’s Roman Galactic Bulge Time Domain Survey (GBTDS). The main focus of the research will be to detect and characterize dark lensing objects such as free-floating planets and black holes on an individual and population level basis. The position is available to start during 2027. Mōu | Who You Are You must have a PhD in Astronomy or a closely related field. Applicants should have strong and demonstrated scientific programming and data analysis skills. Experience working with microlensing data is preferred, although applicants with expertise in other time domain astronomy or large astronomical data sets are also strongly encouraged to apply. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With The Te Kura Matū School of Physical and Chemical Sciences is a thriving community with a legacy spanning 150 years of academic excellence. Amongst the largest Schools within the University, we are driven by our collective commitment to search for innovative solutions that address the problems and challenges both within New Zealand and the World. Our School boasts a vibrant tapestry of individuals, encompassing academic, technical, and administrative professionals from a wide spectrum of backgrounds. United by our shared purpose, we are dedicated to advancing knowledge and pushing the boundaries of scientific discovery. Immersed within the Rutherford Regional Science and Innovation Centre (RRSIC), our laboratories thrive amidst state-of-the-art teaching and research infrastructure. Furthermore, our association with the broader university opens doors to other exceptional research and computational facilities, enhancing the depth of exploration. The School of Physical and Chemical Sciences offers a spectrum of majors including Astronomy, Physics, Chemistry, Biochemistry, Medicinal Chemistry, and Environmental Sciences. Our research endeavours are channelled through five central themes: Sustainability and the Environment, New Materials, Nanotechnology and Laser Physics, Energy and Storage, and Astronomy and Astrophysics, as well as Human, Animal, and Plant Health. By joining our School, you will be a part of pioneering initiatives that shape the future of scientific discovery and leave an indelible mark on the world. For more information about our team, please visit us at Physical and Chemical Sciences | University of Canterbury Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC Tangata Tū, Tangata Ora - Engaged, Empowered, Making a Difference. The University of Canterbury is committed to accessible higher education, service to community and the encouragement of talent without barriers of distance, wealth, class, gender or ethnicity. The University explicitly aims to produce graduates and support staff who are engaged with their communities, empowered to act for good and determined to make a difference in the world. What we offer Flexible working arrangements Supportive working environment Generous annual leave provisions and competitive salary Professional development opportunities A unique working environment in a beautiful campus with access to UC facilities such as the recreation centre and Staff club at discounted rates plus onsite café’s and eateries, and more For more information, please visit: http://www.canterbury.ac.nz/joinus For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit www.canterbury.ac.nz The closing date for this position is: 31 October 2026 (midnight, NZ time) Applications will be reviewed as received and until the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted on our careers website and should include: A cover letter detailing your motivation in applying for the position and your relevant expertise A resume Contact details for two nominated referees Please note, we do not accept applications by email, however we are happy to answer your queries in relation to the application process, please forward these to WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz Prospective candidates are encouraged to contact Associate Professor Michael Albrow ( michael.albrow@canterbury.ac.nz ) for further information or with any questions, please subject the email ‘Postdoctoral fellow in Gravitational Microlensing’. The salary for a Post-Doctoral Fellow for this position is NZ$89,608 p.a. The nominal start is March – September 2027 but some flexibility is possible. Please Note: Candidates who are working towards or nearing completion of a relevant Doctoral qualification, will also be considered. Upon appointment, if the Doctoral qualification is yet to be awarded, the title of Research Associate will be applied and the salary will reflect the Research Associate salary scale. Appointable salary for a Research Associate will be $74,770 p.a. For overseas applicants, please visit Immigration New Zealand website, to review the latest health and visa requirements for visiting and working in New Zealand. Job Details Reference # 34206 Posted on 07 Oct 2026 Closes on 31 Oct 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Administration, Research & Development, Science & Technology Job level(s) Entry/Graduate, Experienced Work type(s) Fixed term full-time More details (document) PD_Gravitational Microlensing.pdf Position description 1 Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 22 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance."
    }
  },
